  "description": "<p>ARCH (Adaptive Reactive CUDA Hydrodynamics) is C++20/CUDA software for finite-volume simulation of compressible fluids, heat transfer and nuclear reactions with adaptive mesh refinement. CPU and CUDA backends share the physical and numerical mathematics. Version 1.3.0 adds the optional ARCH Studio desktop workflow for parameter editing, Configure/Build, real initial-field and initial-AMR previews, Run/Restart, and read-only Plotfile inspection.</p><p>This source deposit is based on the immutable v1.3.0 release commit 3063d883c1d7abf0b6bce5bb31df05af0a49cbac. The prepared Zenodo archive adds only post-release citation, AI-use disclosure and deposit documentation/metadata; ARCHIVE_PROVENANCE.json enumerates those additions and replacements. Scientific source, tests, original acceptance evidence and configuration remain at the tagged version. Git LFS pointers are retained as pointers; required external data and system dependencies must be obtained as described in the build guide. No raw simulation outputs, prebuilt scientific binaries or fetched LFS payloads are added.</p><p>Supported execution is Linux/WSL2; CUDA qualification for this release uses an RTX 3060 Ti (sm86). The published acceptance record maps 16 public models to their applicable evidence, and records 388 Studio tests without skips and the actual desktop workflow. Qualification remains scoped: SNIa uses the accepted joint evidence with the original repeated-AMR FAIL and thermal GAP retained; hotspot functional endpoints do not establish a separate ignition-mechanism result. GNN functionality is outside this release scope. The numerical tolerances and strict floating-point rules remain those of the published acceptance record.</p><p>ARCH-authored material is licensed under MIT. Third-party components, scientific sources and data retain their own terms in LICENSE, THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and LICENSES/.</p><p>AI-assisted tools were used for bug tracking and fixes, review of tolerance settings across the complete CTest suite, editing selected documentation, code completion, and engineering and security checks. The overall software architecture was designed and built by the human contributors. Human maintainers retain responsibility for scientific definitions, numerical acceptance criteria, review, and release decisions.</p>",
